  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Last reviewed 2026-09-15</p>
      <h1>What is Battery Sulfation?</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Sulfation is the most common cause of lead-acid battery death — and the one most people cause by accident.
        It is the hardening of lead sulfate crystals on the plates, and it is the direct result of leaving a battery
        undercharged.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Sulfation is lead sulfate (PbSO₄) crystals that form on a lead-acid battery's
        plates during discharge and then <strong>harden</strong> when the battery is not recharged promptly. Fresh lead
        sulfate converts back to active material on recharge; hardened crystals do not. The result is permanently
        higher internal resistance, lower capacity, and a weaker cold start (lower CCA).
      </div>

      <h2>How sulfation happens</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Discharge</strong> — the chemical reaction turns plate material and sulfuric acid into lead sulfate.</li>
        <li><strong>Prompt recharge</strong> — fresh, fine lead sulfate converts back. This is normal, reversible cycling.</li>
        <li><strong>Left undercharged</strong> — if the battery sits below full, the fine crystals grow into coarse, hard ones that no longer convert back.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The trigger is not age — it is <strong>time spent below full charge</strong>. A battery stored discharged for
        weeks sulfates even if it is never used.
      </p>

      <h2>What sulfation does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Raises internal resistance</strong> — the hard sulfate is a poor conductor.</li>
        <li><strong>Lowers capacity</strong> — less active material is available to react.</li>
        <li><strong>Lowers CCA</strong> — higher resistance means less cranking current, which is why a sulfated battery
        reads weak on a conductance tester and turns the engine slowly.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Prevention and reversal</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What to do</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Prevention</td><td>Recharge promptly; keep the battery charged (float); don't store discharged</td><td>No sulfation</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Early / mild</td><td>A long, slow charge or a controlled equalization (flooded only)</td><td>Partly reversible</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Severe / old</td><td>Replacement — hardened sulfate is permanent</td><td>Not recoverable</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Equalization (a controlled overcharge) can break up mild sulfation in <strong>flooded</strong> batteries, but it
        must not be used on AGM or gel — see
        <a href="/lead-acid/lead-acid-charging/">how to charge a lead-acid battery</a>.
      </p>
      <h2>The sulfation timeline</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Age of the crystals</th><th>State</th><th>Reversible?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hours–days (battery just left discharged)</td><td>Soft, fine crystals</td><td>Mostly — recharge or <a href="/lead-acid/equalization/">equalization</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weeks</td><td>Growing, hardening</td><td>Partially — pulse desulfation may help</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Months+</td><td>Hardened, bonded to plates</td><td>No — capacity is gone (see <a href="/lead-acid/desulfation/">desulfation limits</a>)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Why storage and heat make it worse</h2>
      <p>
        Sulfation runs on two multipliers: <strong>time spent discharged</strong> and <strong>temperature</strong>.
        Heat roughly doubles the chemical reaction rate every 10°C — a battery stored discharged in a hot garage
        sulfates several times faster than one on a cool shelf. The stored-battery rule follows: keep it charged
        and cool (see <a href="/lead-acid/self-discharge/">self-discharge</a>), and check the
        <a href="/lead-acid/battery-date-code/">date code</a> before buying — shelf time is sulfation time.
      </p>


      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>My position:</strong> Sulfation is the battery disease that is almost always a storage and charging
          habit, not a manufacturing defect — and it is cheap to prevent and usually too late to cure.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Supporting logic:</strong> The same battery that dies "suddenly" in winter was usually sulfated quietly over
          months of sitting below full. The cheapest maintenance is simply keeping it charged, which is what the float
          stage exists to do.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the battery regularly kept charged, or does it sit discharged and invite sulfation?</li>
        <li>Would a maintainer or periodic charging prevent the sulfation that is forming?</li>
        <li>Is the sulfation still soft and reversible, or hardened and terminal?</li>
        <li>Does the storage environment (heat, idle) make sulfation worse for this application?</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Sulfation is the lead-acid failure you can prevent — it forms when a battery sits discharged, and it hardens into permanent damage. Keep the battery charged and cool, and the leading cause of early failure never gets a foothold.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — sulfation and lead-acid maintenance references.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer maintenance guides (Yuasa, Canadian Energy, Varta) — sulfation causes, prevention and equalization.</li>
        <li>Cross-verified with charging and failure-mode references.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-15</p>
    </div>
  </section>
